  {
    "episode_id": 90613,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90613.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "First gratitude, count your blessings every day. Look at our blessings. We've been gathered for 40 years plus through thick and thin. We have a daughter we're proud of and we adore. We're fortunate to have a son-in-law who's a world-class human being and two beautiful grandchildren and family and close friends we love. We are a lot to be grateful for. Second, think about the people who don't have what you do and try to make something good happen. The act of trying makes you more optimistic. You literally have the capacity to rewire your brain and to affect your emotions. Pessimism basically becomes a convenient excuse for doing nothing. And if you get caught trying, you'll be happier than if you just sit on the sidelines and let it happen. It's the only position that makes sense. Is it half empty or half full? Truth is it's both. By definition, if it's half filled, it's half empty and half full. Do you see the half full or do you see the half empty? You think, drain that sucker, it's all bad. Are you gonna fill it up? I mean, it's a decision. Optimism is a decision. It's a habit of mine. Do you believe people are basically good or basically bad? I think they're basically good and I think they wanna be better. There are a few people in life who really are gleefully, darkly happy, hurting other people, hogging all the money, hogging all the power, hogging all of this and the other thing. They still wind up disappointed. The happiest people are those that work for the common good. And I've seen it over and over again. I've lived along enough to know. Today, as we do this, it appears that Shimon Peres is on his deathbed in Israel. He has been my friend for 25 years. He lived into his 90s. A lot of people said he was an idealistic dreamer. He devoted his life to painting a picture of a better future. So people could go on, go on through bus bombing, school bombings. endless conflict, and his life was full of grace and goodness is a consequence of it. And everybody, even his opponents were blessed. As long as the opponent was probably Ariel Sharon, who's also sadly passed away, they spent his 80th birthday sitting together with me at a great show in Israel. And Ariel Sharon, by the time a stroke took him, had become an advocate for peace and reconciliation. So, Shimon Perez, people were always skeptical that anyone could be so hopeful. But history, I think, has proved him right. Even when the outcome was not what he agreed with, he lived to be over 90 because he was a happy warrior, because he chose to think of how things could be in a positive way, not how bad they are today. It's a choice we all make. I recommend the choice.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
